Com$ adapt GF 09 2020


1,0$ source  : A revision of Collybia s.l. in the northeastern United States & adjacent Canada ///http://www.nybg.org/bsci/res/col/sectkey.html



1,cs1 $ Spore deposit pinkish cream to pinkish buff when fresh; some spores cyanophilous and dextrinoid; basidiocarps typically putrescent and fleshy $$s" Rhodocollybia"
1,2 $ Spore deposit white to ivory yellow or buff, rarely pink; spores neither cyanophilous nor dextrinoid; basidiocarps membranous to marcescent
2,3$ Basidiomata white to gray, typically arising from a sclerotium or blackened fungous remains, rarely in humus, with filiform stipe and small pileus (<20 mm); spores typically less than 6 x 3 µm
2,cs4 $ Basidiomata neither arising from a sclerotium nor blackened fungous remains, if in humus, stipe never filiform and with pileus >20 mm; spores usually >6 µm $ $s"Gymnopus"

3,cs2 $ Stipe producing narrow side branchlets with conidia; sclerotia black to dark gray $$s" Dendrocollybia"
3,cs3 $ Stipe lacking side branchlets; sclerotia dark yellow, orange to reddish brown or absent $$s" Collybia"



cs1,0 $ Spore deposit pinkish cream to pinkish buff when fresh; some spores cyanophilous and dextrinoid; basidiocarps typically putrescent and fleshy $$s" Rhodocollybia"

1,2 $ Pileipellis hyphae repent and radially oriented; taste mild or not distinctive
1,5 $ Pileipellis hyphae a tangled trichodermium at first, becoming repent and interwoven with age; taste bitter
2,0 $ Spores globose. $ R. prolixa var. distorta (eastern USA) R. badiialba (western USA, IMAGE ONLY )
2,3 $ Spores ellipsoid to lacrymoid
3,0 $ Spores (6.2-)7-9(-10.5) x 3.5-4.2(-4.8) µm; stipe clavate $ R. butyracea (including f. asema with gray brown colors)
3,4 $ Spores 5-7.5 x 3-4.5 µm; stipe equal, sometimes subradicating
4,0 $ Cheilocystidia absent; lamellar edges distinctly serrate from the beginning; stipe notsubradicating $ R. lentinoides
4,0 $ Cheilocystidia present; lamellar edges laciniate or serrate only with age; stipe subradicating inrotten wood $ R. unakensis
5,0 $ Spores globose to subglobose (5.6-6.4(-7) x 4.8-5.6 µm) $ R. maculata var. maculata
5,6 $ Spores ellipsoid to lacrymoid (5.6-7 x 3.5-4.2 µm)
6,0 $ Lamellae whitish, lacking yellow colors $ R. maculata var. occidentalis
6,0 $ Lamellae yellow (stipe sometimes yellow) $ R. maculata var. scorzonerea



cs2,0 $ Stipe producing narrow side branchlets with conidia; sclerotia black to dark gray $$ s" Dendrocollybia"
1,0$$Dendrocollybia racemosa (Pers.:Fr.) Redhead & Petersen

cs3,0 $ Stipe lacking side branchlets; sclerotia dark yellow, orange to reddish brown or absent $$s" Collybia"

1,0 $ Stipe racemose (i.e., with short side branches); sclerotium black $ Dendrocollybia racemosa (IMAGE ONLY) (California, Oregon, Washington)
1,2 $ Stipe not racemose; sclerotium brown, orange to yellow, if present
2,0 $ Basidiocarps not arising from a sclerotium, usually growing from old agaric remains, rarely on humus $ Collybia cirrhata
2,3 $ Basidiocarps arising from a sclerotium (may be buried in the substrate)
3,0 $ Sclerotium lacrymoid, ellipsoid to ovoid, dark red brown; basidiocarps mostly on rotten agarics, rarely on soil or humus; cheilocystidia present but inconspicuous and scattered; pileocystidia absent $ Collybia tuberosa
3,0 $ Sclerotium globose to subglobose, pale yellow to pale ochraceous orange; basidiocarps mostly on soil or humus, sometimes on agarics; cheilocystidia absent; pileocystidia present $ Collybia cookei


cs4,0 $ Basidiomata neither arising from a sclerotium nor blackened fungous remains, if in humus, stipe never filiform and with pileus >20 mm; spores usually >6 µm $ $
s"Gymnopus"
1,cs6 $ Pileus with violet pigments which become blue in alkali $$s" section Iocephalae"
1,2 $ Pileus without violet pigments; reaction to alkali is various, but not blue
2,cs7 $ Hyphae of the pileus surface repent (end cells rarely suberect), generally radially oriented, cylindric; diverticulate elements and branchlets sometimes present $ $$s"section Vestipedes
2,3 $ Hyphae of the pileus surface frequently branched, with short, broad cells without radial orientation
3,cs8 $ Stipe strongly fusoid, deeply radicating on or around Quercus (rarely Betula or Fagus) roots and stumps $ $$s"section Striipedes (Collybia fusipes, Old World) IMAGE ONLY
3,cs9 $ Stipe not strongly fusoid and radicating, but sometimes on Quercus $$$s" section Levipedes


cs6,0 $ Pileus with violet pigments which become blue in alkali $$s" section Iocephalae"


cs7,0 $ Hyphae of the pileus surface repent (end cells rarely suberect), generally radially oriented, cylindric; diverticulate elements and branchlets sometimes present $ $$s"section Vestipedes
1,0 $ Spores globose $ Collybia subdryophila (extralimital)
1,2 $ Spores ellipsoid to lacrymoid
2,3 $ Spores greater than 3 µm broad
2,25 $ Spores 2-3 µm broad
3,0 $ Basidiomata densely cespitose with dull, glabrous stipes $ G. acervatus
3,4 $ Basidiomata usually not densely cespitose; stipe pruinose, densely tomentose, pubescent or fibrous and conspicuously twisted striate
4,0 $ Stipe typically greater than 5 mm thick, often curved, conspicuously twisted and fibrillose striate; lamellae broad $ G. luxurians
4,5 $ Stipe usually not greater than 5 mm thick, usually straight and stiff, tough, densely tomentose to pruinose or pubescent; lamellae narrow, or if broad, then colored yellow and taste acrid
5,0 $ Taste bitter, sometimes absent; pileus dark brown, not hygrophanous, fading only slightly in color, rugulose sulcate toward the margin; lamellae subdistant to distant when drying out $ G. subnudus
5,6 $ Taste mild or acrid, never bitter; pileus brown to yellow brown at first, hygrophanous, even to subsulcate at margin; lamellae very crowded or distant
6,0 $ Taste acrid; yellow colors predominant; lamellae distant and broad $ G. peronatus (Oregon and Washington) IMAGE ONLY
6,7 $ Taste never acrid, mild or foetid to alliaceous; yellow colors absent; interlamellar spacing variable
7,8 $ Cheilocystidia present
7,12 $ Cheilocystidia absent
8,0 $ Stipe base a tuberculate knob; cheilocystidia saccate and voluminous $ G. dichrous
8,9 $ Stipe base not ending in a tuberculate knob; cheilocystidia typically diverticulate, cylindric, flexuous or narrowly clavate
9,0 $ Stipe surface with a tawny vesture toward the base; odor and taste mild or not distinctive $ G. biformis
9,10 $ Stipe surface with a pallid vesture above and below; odor and taste alliaceous or not distinctive
10,11 $ Spores less than 9 µm; odor and taste alliaceous
10,105 $ Spores longer than 9 µm; odor and taste not distinctive
11,0 $ Pileus surface even or sometimes wavy at the margin, but not sulcate or plicate; lamellae crowded; spores 7 µm long or less; growing on leaf litter $ G. polyphyllus
11,0 $ Pileus surface plicate to sulcate striate; lamellae distant; spores greater than 7 µm long; growing on wood chips $ G. dysodes
12,0 $ Caulocystidia very thick walled, often obscuring the cell lumen $ G. contrarius
12,13 $ Caulocystidia not thick walled
13,14 $ Spores greater than 7 µm long; odor lacking or not alliaceous
13,15 $ Spores less than 7 µm long; odor alliaceous
14,0 $ Lamellae brown AND close; hyphae of pileipellis subgelatinous and arising from a narrow zone of radially oriented, conspicuously encrusted cells $ G. putillus
14,0 $ Lamellae white to pinkish buff, interlamellar distance variable; hyphae of pileipellis not gelatinous, encrusted cells present, scattered, inconspicuous, but not in a well-defined zone $ G. subterginus
15,0 $ Pileus brownish red; lamellae distant $ G. impudicus (Wisconsin, Michigan, New York)
15,0 $ Pileus whitish; lamellae close $ G. hariolorum (SE USA)
25,0 $ Stipe arising from litter, humus, or wood but not inserted; lamellae adnexed and not fused into a collar $ G. striatipes
25,0 $ Stipe base inserted on wood; lamellae forming a collar around the stipe apex $ G. fasciatus
105,0 $ Lamellae close to subdistant $ G. terginus
105,0 $ Lamellae very crowded $ G. confluens



cs8,0 $ Stipe strongly fusoid, deeply radicating on or around Quercus (rarely Betula or Fagus) roots and stumps $ $$s"section Striipedes (Collybia fusipes, Old World) IMAGE ONLY
1,0$Basidiomata marcescent, tough, or membranous, rarely putrescent and fleshy; spore deposit white to cream colored; spores inamyloid, acyanophilous.$Gymnopus fusipes (Bull.:Fr.) S. F. Gray

cs9,0 $ Stipe not strongly fusoid and radicating, but sometimes on Quercus $$$s" section Levipedes
1,2 $ Some portion of the basidiomata turning green with alkali
1,5 $ Basidiomata not green with alkali
2,0 $ Hyphae of the lamellar trama with coarse, dark brown pigment granules seen in mounts of water or Melzer's reagent $ G. alkalivirens (eastern USA) & G. fuscopurpureus (western USA)
2,3 $ Hyphae of the lamellar trama without coarse pigment granules
3,0 $ Hyphae of the stipe trama with dark brown granular pigment encrustations in mounts of water or Melzer's; stipe with tawny strigosity only toward the base $ G. semihirtipes
3,4 $ Hyphae of the stipe trama without dark pigment encrustions in water mounts or Melzer's; stipe glabrous and dark red brown OR tawny and strigose overall
4,0 $ Stipe glabrous and dark red brown $ G. erythropus
4,0 $ Stipe tawny and strigose overall $ G. spongiosus
5,0 $ Growing on soil in the woods, or on lawns, pastures, etc., appearing in early spring $ G. earleae
5,6 $ Growing on leaf litter, rotten wood, etc, fruiting season variable
6,0 $ Stipe pubescent to tomentose from apex to base; pileus dull sordid tan when fresh; basidiomata with a marcescent texture $ G. kauffmanii
6,7 $ Stipe base usually glabrous but occasionally thinly, white pruinose with age; pileus dark reddish brown to honey yellow when fresh; texture somewhat fleshy fibrous
7,0 $ Rhizomorphs pinkish ochre; basidiomata with yellow colors $ C. subsulphurea (see discussion under G. dryophilus)
7,8 $ Rhizomorphs white; basidiomata dark reddish brown
8,0 $ Cheilocystidia filamentous; basidiomata on litter, rotted wood; lamellae crowded $ G. dryophilus
8,0 $ Cheilocystidia inflated; basidiomata on wood mulch or soil in spring; lamellae close $ C. brunneola (see discussion under G. dryophilus)




